        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mahabharata</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 400 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Traditionally ascribed to the sage Vyasa, himself a major figure in the epic; it began as an orally transmitted tale of charioteer bards and grew through several redactions, its bulk probably compiled between the 3rd century BCE and the 3rd century CE, its final form reached by the early Gupta period.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> epic, the Mahābhārata (“Great Bharata”), in 18 parvas or books, told as tales within tales; its longest version has over 100,000 shlokas (couplets), or over 200,000 lines, with long prose passages, about 1.8 million words in all.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and abroad, the oldest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> text dating to the Kushan Period; the Critical Edition of the Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, on 13,000 pages in 19 volumes; translations and retellings in Tamil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, Telugu, Bengali, Persian, Javanese and many other languages.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the rivalry of the Kauravas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a>; the dice game and the thirteen-year exile; the 18-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> War; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s teaching to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>; dharmayuddha, the theory of just war; the stories of Shakuntala, of Savitri and Satyavan and of Damayanti</span></div>
