    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ascribed by tradition to Vyasa, the Mahabharata grew from an oral tale of bards into one of the longest epic poems known, its bulk probably compiled between the 3rd century BCE and the 3rd century CE.</li><li>It tells of the struggle between the Kaurava and Pandava cousins for the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastinapura">Hastinapura</a>, ending in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> War, and holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, a prominent sacred text in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> traditions.</li><li>Retold in Tamil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, Telugu, Bengali and many other languages, in Persian at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s court and in Javanese, it lives on in drama, film, television and folk ritual, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Gita</a> helped inspire the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At about 1.8 million words, the Mahabharata is roughly ten times the length of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> combined.</p></aside>
    </section>

