    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">4th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>'s grammar, the Ashtadhyayi, gives the earliest known references to bharata and mahabharata, though whether it means the epic is uncertain</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century BCE – 3rd century CE</span><span class="rf-text">The bulk of the Mahabharata is probably compiled in these centuries</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 4th century CE</span><span class="rf-text">The text probably reaches its final form by the early Gupta period</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">533–534 CE</span><span class="rf-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> Sharvanatha from Khoh describes the epic as a collection of 100,000 verses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">16th century</span><span class="rf-text">Faizi and ʽAbd al-Qadir Badayuni produce the Razmnameh, a Persian translation commissioned by the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1883–1896</span><span class="rf-text">Kisari Mohan Ganguli's prose version, the first complete English translation, is published</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919–1966</span><span class="rf-text">Scholars at the Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> produce the Critical Edition of the Mahabharata</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1988</span><span class="rf-text">B. R. Chopra's television series Mahabharat is broadcast on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s national television, Doordarshan</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vedas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vedas.jpg" alt="Vedas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda,_manuscript_page_sample_i,_Mandala_1,_Hymn_1_(Sukta_1),_Adhyaya_1,_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ms Sarah Welch, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mahabharata's setting has a historical precedent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> (Vedic) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a> was the centre of political power during roughly 1200 to 800 BCE, and some elements of the epic can be traced back to Vedic times. A dynastic conflict of that period could have inspired the Jaya, the foundation of the corpus, with a climactic battle that came to be viewed as an epochal event. The historicity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> War is unclear; many historians date it to the 10th century BCE, and F. E. Pargiter's reckoning from Puranic genealogies gives about 950 BCE. Indian tradition holds other dates: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata">Aryabhata</a>'s 3102 BCE has become widespread, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> implies 3137 BCE, and Varāhamihira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a> place the war around 2449 BCE.</p><p>The epic started as an orally transmitted tale of the charioteer bards. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, which had to be preserved letter-perfect, it was a popular work whose reciters followed changes in language and style, so its earliest surviving parts are believed to be no older than the earliest outside references to it. It drew on Vedic literature: the snake sacrifice of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janamejaya">Janamejaya</a> has a close connection to Vedic (Brahmana) literature, and the Panchavimsha Brahmana names Dhritarashtra and Janamejaya among the priests of such a sacrifice, and the Suparnakhyana, a late Vedic poem counted among the earliest traces of epic poetry in India, is a shorter precursor of the Garuda legend in the Adi Parva.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pandavas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pandavas.jpg" alt="Pandavas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Draupadi_and_Pandavas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Raja Ravi Varma, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The core story is a dynastic struggle for the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastinapura">Hastinapura</a>, ruled by the Kuru clan, between two branches of the family: the Kauravas, the hundred sons of the blind king Dhritarashtra led by Duryodhana, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a>, the five sons of Pandu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira">Yudhishthira</a>, Bhima, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>, Nakula and Sahadeva, who all marry the princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a>. The Pandavas escape a plot to burn them in a house of lac and build a new capital at Indraprastha, but Yudhishthira loses everything, his brothers, himself and Draupadi, in a dice game against Duryodhana's uncle Shakuni, and the Pandavas are sent into exile for 12 years, with a 13th year in disguise. When talks fail, the two sides meet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a>, and after an 18-day battle the Pandavas are victorious.</p><p>Before the battle Arjuna, seeing his kin and teachers in the opposing army, refuses to fight, and his charioteer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> reminds him of his duty as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a>; this dialogue is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>. The epic ends with the death of Krishna, the end of his dynasty and the Pandavas' ascent to heaven, and marks the beginning of the Kali Yuga. Along the way it tells many other stories, among them those of Shakuntala, of Savitri and Satyavan and of Damayanti, and an abbreviated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, and it discusses the four goals of life and dharmayuddha, just war, with rules such as the fair treatment of captives and the wounded.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ganesha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganesha.jpg" alt="Ganesha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganesha_Basohli_miniature_circa_1730_Dubost_p73.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The epic is traditionally ascribed to the sage Vyasa, who is also a major figure in it. Its first section says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> wrote the text down to Vyasa's dictation, but scholars regard this as a later interpolation, and the Critical Edition does not include it. The story is told as a frame tale: Vaisampayana, a disciple of Vyasa, first recites it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janamejaya">Janamejaya</a>, great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>, and many years later the storyteller Ugrashrava Sauti recites it again to sages at a twelve-year sacrifice in the Naimisha Forest.</p><p>Research has put an enormous effort into dating the text's layers. The epic itself distinguishes a core of 24,000 verses, the Bharata proper, and at least three redactions are commonly recognised: the Jaya of 8,800 verses attributed to Vyasa, the Bharata of 24,000 recited by Vaisampayana, and the Mahabharata of over 100,000 recited by Ugrashrava Sauti, though John Brockington argues that Jaya and Bharata are the same text. The redaction followed formal principles that emphasised the numbers 18 and 12. The bulk was probably compiled between the 3rd century BCE and the 3rd century CE, with the oldest preserved parts not much older than around 400 BCE, and the text probably reached its final form by the early Gupta period (c. 4th century CE); a mention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Huna</a> implies that the Bhishma Parva may have been edited around the 4th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/andhra-mahabharatam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andhra-mahabharatam.jpg" alt="Andhra Mahabharatam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/andhra-mahabharatam">Andhra Mahabharatam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bazaar_art_print_of_Bhavadgita,_c._mid-1900%27s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bazaar art, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Early references show the epic's spread. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>'s grammar of the 4th century BCE knows the words bharata and mahabharata and some of the epic's figures, though whether it refers to the epic itself is uncertain. The Greek writer Dio Chrysostom (c. 40 – c. 120 CE) reported that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s poetry was sung even in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, which many scholars take as evidence of a Mahabharata at that date. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> Sharvanatha (533–534 CE) from Khoh calls it a “collection of 100,000 verses”. Its stories took on lives of their own in Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a>'s Abhijnanashakuntala draws on one, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhasa">Bhasa</a>'s play Urubhanga on the slaying of Duryodhana by Bhima. Within the Indian tradition it is sometimes called the fifth Veda.</p><p>It was translated and retold across India and beyond: in Tamil from the 9th century, in the Javanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Kawi</a> Mahabharata of the late 10th century, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Pampa</a> and Kumaravyasa, in the Telugu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-mahabharatam">Andhra Mahabharatam</a> of the 11th to 14th centuries, in Bengali, Odia, Assamese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, Punjabi and Hindi, and in Persian, first for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Zayn al-Abidin and then in the Razmnameh commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>. Jain authors told their own versions, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> is reborn in hell for his exploits but will one day be reborn as a Tirthankara. Some early 20th-century Indologists judged the text chaotic: Hermann Oldenberg called the full text a “horrible chaos”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/virata"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/virata.jpg" alt="Virata" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/virata">Virata</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virata%27s_Palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ravi Varma Press, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscript</a> evidence for the epic is somewhat late, given its materials and the climate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, but very extensive. The oldest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> text dates to the Kushan Period (200 CE), and the absence of the Anushasana Parva and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virata">Virata</a> Parva from the “Spitzer manuscript” helps to date the latest additions. Because the text was fluid, Vishnu Sukthankar, editor of the first great critical edition, held that it was useless to seek an original shape; the aim could only be “to reconstruct the oldest form of the text which it is possible to reach based on the manuscript material available.”</p><p>Between 1919 and 1966 scholars at the Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, compared manuscripts from India and abroad and produced the Critical Edition, on 13,000 pages in 19 volumes, with the Harivamsha in two more volumes and six index volumes; it is the text usually used in Mahabharata studies today. Complete English versions include Kisari Mohan Ganguli's Victorian prose (1883–1896), now in the public domain, P. Lal's verse rendering of all slokas in all recensions, and Bibek Debroy's unabridged ten volumes (2010–2014). A prose translation based on the Critical Edition, begun by J. A. B. van Buitenen and continued by several scholars after his death, is still in progress at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> Press, and the Clay Sanskrit Library has published 15 volumes of a projected 32.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita.jpg" alt="Bhagavad Gita" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BhagavadGita-19th-century-Illustrated-Sanskrit-Chapter_1.20.21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> explains to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> his duties as a warrior and prince and sets out Yogic and Vedantic philosophies, and the Gita is often described as a concise guide to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> philosophy and a practical guide to life. In modern times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Netaji</a> Subhas Chandra Bose, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and many others used it to help inspire the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement. The epic's discussion of dharmayuddha is one of the first instances of theorising about just war, setting out standards of proportionality, just means, just cause and the treatment of captives and the wounded that would later be debated across the world.</p><p>Modern writers keep returning to it: Ramdhari Singh Dinkar's Rashmirathi (1952), centred on Karna, Dharamvir Bharati's play Andha Yug (1955), Pratibha Ray's novel Yajnaseni (1984) and Chitra Banerjee Divakaruni's The Palace of Illusions (2008), both told from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a>'s perspective, and a 1,260-page comic book version by Amar Chitra Katha. Film versions date back to 1920; Peter Brook's nine-hour play premiered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> in 1985, and B. R. Chopra's television series Mahabharat was shown on Doordarshan in 1988. Every year villagers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a> perform the Pandav Lila, a ritual re-enactment of episodes from the epic through dancing, singing and recitation.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Mahabharata, traditionally ascribed to the sage Vyasa, is one of the two major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> epics of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>: the story of the war between the Kaurava and Pandava cousins, framed with many other tales and teachings, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>. Grown over centuries to about 1.8 million words, it is revered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and has been retold in many languages and media.</em></p>

